+
+# Enterprise UI Governance Contract
+
+**Status:** Mandatory. Every screen, component, and feature page must comply.
+**Applies to:** shell, main canvas, feature pages, utility panels, forms, tables, dialogs, empty/loading/error states.
+**Supersedes:** ad-hoc values. The narrative rationale lives in a separate design document; **this file is the enforced contract**. Where the two differ, this file wins.
+**Enforcement:** A CI build step (e.g., `ui-contract.test.ts`) fails on violations of the token, wording, and surface rules below.
+
+---
+
+## 0. The six laws
+
+1. **No raw values.** Never write a pixel, weight, radius, or font size literal in component CSS when a token exists. If a value is missing, add a token — do not invent a one-off.
+2. **Dense but calm.** This is a professional enterprise product. Default density is compact; whitespace is structural, never decorative padding.
+3. **Consistency beats cleverness.** A new page must look like it shipped the same day as every other page.
+4. **Touch expands, desktop compacts.** Compact desktop sizing is the default; coarse pointers get full touch targets automatically.
+5. **RTL is a first-class layout, not a translation.** Every rule here must hold in right-to-left scripts.
+6. **Compose from shared blocks; never re-implement one.** If two screens need the same thing, it belongs in the shared component library. Copy-pasted UI is how a product stops looking like one product.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Research basis
+
+These values are not invented. They are the intersection of published guidance for information-dense enterprise UI:
+
+| Source | What it establishes | How this system applies it |
+|---|---|---|
+| IBM Carbon — spacing & data table | 8px base with 2px mini-steps (2/4/8/12/16/24/32/40/48); table densities 24/32/40/48/64px | Our spacing scale is identical; our default table row is 32px |
+| Atlassian Design — spacing | 0–8px for compact UI, 12–24px for component padding, 32–80px for layout | Maps to `space-1..2`, `space-3..6`, `space-8..16` |
+| Microsoft Fluent — Standard vs Compact | Information-rich UI needs an explicit compact mode, ~14px body | 14px body, compact 32px controls as default |
+| WCAG 2.2 SC 2.5.8 (AA) | Interactive targets ≥ **24×24 CSS px** | `--ui-hit-min: 24px`; nothing interactive is smaller |
+| WCAG 2.2 SC 2.5.5 (AAA) / Material 3 | 44px / 48dp for touch | `--ui-control-touch: 48px`, applied automatically at `pointer: coarse` |
+| Dense-dashboard practice (industry standard) | 8–12px grid gaps, 12-column grid, 12–14px type, sticky headers, right-aligned numerics | Our grid gap, type scale and table rules |
+| Nielsen Norman / dashboard research | 4–6 primary metrics above the fold before cognitive load degrades decisions | KPI rows are capped at 6 cards |
+
+**Deliberate deviation, documented:** Any specific custom width (e.g., a dense left rail) must be explicitly justified and flagged as "do not correct" to prevent well-intentioned but harmful refactoring.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Token reference — the only allowed values
+
+Defined in `src/styles/tokens.css`. **This table is the contract.**
+
+### 2.1 Spacing
+
+| Token | px | Use |
+|---|---:|---|
+| `--ui-space-0` | 0 | flush joins only |
+| `--ui-space-05` | 2 | hairline offsets, tightest label/value pairs |
+| `--ui-space-1` | 4 | icon↔label gap, chip internals, field label→control |
+| `--ui-space-2` | 8 | compact gaps, toolbar item gap, badge padding |
+| `--ui-space-3` | 12 | compact card padding, form field stack gap, button x-padding |
+| `--ui-space-4` | 16 | standard card/panel/modal padding |
+| `--ui-space-5` | 20 | dense section vertical rhythm |
+| `--ui-space-6` | 24 | comfortable section padding, modal backdrop inset |
+| `--ui-space-8` | 32 | major group separation |
+| `--ui-space-10` | 40 | major vertical break |
+| `--ui-space-12` | 48 | page section separation |
+| `--ui-space-16` | 64 | rare top-level grouping |
+
+Never use 6px, 10px, 14px, 18px as spacing. Round to the scale.
+
+**Nested containers must not repeat the same gap.** Spacing compounds: a 16px section gap inside a 16px form gap inside a 16px row gap reads as one enormous gap, not as structure. Step the scale down as you nest, so hierarchy comes from *contrast* between levels:
+
+| Nesting level | Gap | Meaning |
+|---|---:|---|
+| Between sections | `space-3` (12) | Separate topics |
+| Between rows in a section | `space-3` (12) | Separate settings |
+| Between a label block and its controls | `space-2` (8) | One unit |
+| Between fields in a grid | `space-2` row / `space-3` column | Peers |
+| Between a label and its own control | `space-1` (4) | Inseparable |
+
+Rule of thumb: **each level inward is one step smaller, never equal or larger.** If a block feels airy, count the nested gaps before adding anything new — the fix is almost always removing a duplicated gap, not adding a divider.
+
+### 2.2 Control heights
+
+| Token | px | Use |
+|---|---:|---|
+| `--ui-hit-min` | 24 | absolute floor for any interactive target (WCAG AA) |
+| `--ui-control-xs` | 24 | inline/table-row actions, small buttons |
+| `--ui-control-compact` | 32 | **desktop default** — buttons, inputs, selects, toolbar controls |
+| `--ui-control-standard` | 40 | primary form controls, dialog primary actions, surface header min-height |
+| `--ui-control-touch` | 48 | applied automatically under `@media (pointer: coarse)` |
+
+### 2.3 Control widths
+
+A control's width is a usability cue: it tells the user how much input is expected. Stretching every field to fill its row throws that cue away.
+
+| Token | px | Use |
+|---|---:|---|
+| `--ui-field-width-xs` | 96 | Codes, quantities, currency codes |
+| `--ui-field-width-sm` | 140 | Short pickers, e.g. a status filter |
+| `--ui-field-width-md` | 176 | **Default filter width.** Fits ~18 characters |
+| `--ui-field-width-lg` | 240 | Longer pickers and compact text inputs |
+| `--ui-field-width-search` | 320 | Search fields |
+
+Rules:
+
+1. **Match the width to the expected input length.** Mismatched widths cause measurable hesitation.
+2. **Cap, do not stretch.** Set a max width instead of letting a control grow with the viewport.
+3. Form fields inside a field group may fill their grid cell; **toolbar controls may not**.
+4. Below 640px controls may fill, because a stacked layout no longer carries a width cue to lose.
+
+### 2.4 Table rows
